<article class="env-definition" id="011Q">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/011Q">Definition <span data-tag="011Q">12.21.1</span>.</a> Let $\mathcal{A}$ be an abelian category. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
An <em>exact couple</em> is a datum $(A, E, \alpha , f, g)$ where $A$, $E$ are objects of $\mathcal{A}$ and $\alpha $, $f$, $g$ are morphisms as in the following diagram 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix{ A \ar[rr]_{\alpha } &  &  A \ar[ld]^ g \\  &  E \ar[lu]^ f &  }  \]
</div>
<p>
 with the property that the kernel of each arrow is the image of its predecessor. So $\mathop{\mathrm{Ker}}(\alpha ) = \mathop{\mathrm{Im}}(f)$, $\mathop{\mathrm{Ker}}(f) = \mathop{\mathrm{Im}}(g)$, and $\mathop{\mathrm{Ker}}(g) = \mathop{\mathrm{Im}}(\alpha )$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
A <em>morphism of exact couples</em> $t : (A, E, \alpha , f, g) \to (A', E', \alpha ', f', g')$ is given by morphisms $t_ A : A \to A'$ and $t_ E : E \to E'$ such that $\alpha ' \circ t_ A = t_ A \circ \alpha $, $f' \circ t_ E = t_ A \circ f$, and $g' \circ t_ A = t_ E \circ g$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article>